--TEST--
`put` over a key that already holds a value replaces it — the read after every write in a sweep answers that write, the shape may change with it, and thirteen rewrites totalling more than a shard's share of the cap forget nothing that was written before them — `rule:core-api/two-cache-tiers`, `rule:concurrency/a-cached-value-is-copied-across-the-boundary` and `rule:concurrency/the-memory-tier-is-one-store-per-process`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:concurrency/put-and-get-are-the-whole-boundary` gives the store two
// operations and no third one, so there is no spelling
// of an append, a merge or an add-if-absent: a `put` over a live key is the
// whole of what happens to that key. The sweep below is what a store keeping
// the *first* write, or holding a longer value and overwriting it in place,
// would fail while answering plausibly on any single line.
var $cache = Core\Cache::memory();

// Written before every rewrite, so they are the oldest keys in the tier and the
// first ones the cap would forget. The tier evicts per shard, and which shard a
// key lands in is a hash's business, so there are enough of them that the shard
// "hot" lands in holds some of them too. Nothing below names them again.
int $steadyCount = 256;
for (int $i = 0; $i < $steadyCount; $i = $i + 1) {
    $cache->put("steady-" . ($i as string), "s");
}

// The sweep alternates the length in both directions and writes a value it has
// already written: a store truncating to the incoming length would pass the
// growing half, one padding to the length it held would pass the shrinking
// half, and one treating the empty string as an absence would pass all but two.
array<string> $values = [
    "first",
    "",
    Core\Str::repeat("long ", 560),
    "short again",
    Core\Str::repeat("x", 2700),
    Core\Str::repeat("ab", 1300),
    "first",
    Core\Str::repeat("y", 2500),
    "",
    Core\Str::repeat("z", 2400),
    Core\Str::repeat("mid ", 600),
    Core\Str::repeat("q", 2300),
    "the last one",
];

tainted string $back = "";
int $answered = 0;
foreach ($values as string $value) {
    $cache->put("hot", $value);
    $back = $cache->get("hot") as string;
    if ($back == $value) {
        $answered = $answered + 1;
    }
}
echo "the read after each write answered that write: ", $answered, " of ", Core\Arr::count($values), "\n";

// The replacement is of the entry and not of its bytes, so the shape a key
// holds is the shape of the last value put there — the graph copy runs over
// whatever arrived, with nothing of the key's previous payload in it.
$cache->put("shape", "text first");
$cache->put("shape", 7);
int $flipped = $cache->get("shape") as int;
echo "a rewrite replaces the shape as well: ", $flipped, "\n";

// And a rewrite costs the tier one entry rather than one per write. Those
// thirteen writes total more than one shard's share of the cap below, while the
// largest two of them and the shard's steady keys fit under it together, so a
// store that added an entry per write would have had to forget its oldest keys —
// and the oldest keys are the ones written before the sweep started.
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/cache.rs`'s `order` queue is where that is decided: a
// slot per live key, in the order each was **first** written, so an overwrite
// keeps its place and leaves nothing behind it.
int $kept = 0;
for (int $j = 0; $j < $steadyCount; $j = $j + 1) {
    if ($cache->get("steady-" . ($j as string)) != null) {
        $kept = $kept + 1;
    }
}
echo "the keys written before every one of those rewrites: ", $kept, " of ", $steadyCount, " kept\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The tier's cap (`rule:concurrency/the-memory-tier-is-one-store-per-process`).
# The tier is cut into 64 shards that each evict against an equal
# share of it, so this is a share of 16K: low enough that the sweep's own bytes
# would not fit under it, and high enough that the two largest entries fit side
# by side with the steady keys — which is what an overwrite needs, since the cap
# prices the incoming entry before it takes out the one being replaced.
[cache.memory]
max_size = "1M"
--EXPECT--
the read after each write answered that write: 13 of 13
a rewrite replaces the shape as well: 7
the keys written before every one of those rewrites: 256 of 256 kept
